    "body": "fix on issue/33-c-unwind · PR #860 (draft, `Refs #33`) · pinned by c_axis_unwind::{a_rotary_contour_on_a_stated_c_travel_unwinds_at_its_links_and_posts, a_rewind_through_stock_is_convicted_by_the_verifier, a_ring_wider_than_the_stated_travel_refuses_by_name, two_rings_unwound_post_to_their_golden}, cl::rotary_unwind::tests (3) · merges at the next cut\n\nSlice 2 covers owed item 2: unwinding C on links against a stated travel.\n\n`cl::unwind_c` brings a C-stamped path inside the machine's C travel by whole turns. It does this only at clear links, meaning points reached by a rapid at the path's own clearance that are strictly beyond every cut. Each rewind is written into the path as the part-frame orbit it is (rapid chords, each with its own C), so verify, the solid bar audit, the steel laws and the post all see it. A test shows that a rewind through stock is convicted as RapidInStock. A cut whose own C span cannot fit the travel is refused by name at generation. On the shipped millturn.mch (C -360..360), a multi-ring Rotary Contour now generates, verifies and posts. An unstated travel changes nothing.\n\nGated on this branch after #848 merged: the quick gate fell back to batch scope because cl changed, and all 9 steps are green, including clippy -D warnings.\n\nStill owed: 1 (C-sense word), 3 (axial/face stations, G12.1), 4 (arcs on a C turn), 5 (feed law on a real control), 6 (live_tool words in a shipped machine). A control roll-over word is a possible alternative to orbit rewinds; see docs/decisions/33-c-unwind.md.",
